WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.500
The post-credit scene has become one of the
hallmark features of the MCU — which isn't

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.889
surprising, since every instalment in the
franchise has at least one.

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:14.410
But these sequences do more than just advertise
future Marvel movies.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:18.289
Here's why the post-credit scenes are the
best part of the MCU.

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:22.540
Marvel uses their end-credit scenes for a
number of different purposes.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:27.880
Some, of course, are serious and somber — but
one of the best uses for a good end-credit

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.249
clip is as a humor-laced palette cleanser.

00:00:31.249 --> 00:00:37.000
For obvious reasons, superhero flicks typically
go high-stakes during the third act, and that

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.579
means things can get pretty serious pretty
quick.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:45.559
Villains make bold moves, heroes overcome
their personal shortcomings, and the action

00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:48.930
gets cranked up to 11.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:54.870
A post-credit scene is an ideal way to keep
things light as the story wraps up.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:01:01.109
For example, a quick trip to the Shawarma
Palace was the perfect way to ease the tension-filled

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:03.210
final act of The Avengers.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:08.090
Bruce Banner's role as a sleepy therapist
played off nicely against Tony Stark's weighty

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.500
self-revelations in Iron Man 3.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:16.570
Even in a more comedic movie like Guardians
of the Galaxy, the sapling Groot's sneaky

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:21.390
jive to "I Want You Back" just keeps the good
times rolling.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:26.409
There are many other, often more serious,
reasons that the MCU's arsenal of post-credit

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:29.750
scenes are counted among the best parts of
these films.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:35.120
And yet, even if there was literally no narrative
substance to be found in them, their comedic

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.230
nature alone would still make them invaluable
additions to the MCU canon.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:45.970
The end of a movie is typically meant to bring
a sense of clarity and closure.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:51.700
Storylines are wrapped up, mysteries are solved,
and a sense of normalcy tends to be restored.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:57.409
However, when it comes to a serial universe
like the MCU, everyone already knows that

00:01:57.409 --> 00:02:02.160
the tranquility is only temporary and, often,
circumstantial.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.219
This is where post-credit scenes come in to
pack a punch — by relentlessly stirring

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:10.660
up a constant sense of uncertainty for the
future.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:15.150
As much as viewers might want to rest easy
knowing that Peter Parker put Adrian Toomes

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:21.120
behind bars, the villain's post-credit run-in
with Mac Gargan shows that Spidey's duel with

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.760
Vulture is anything but over.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.290
Meanwhile, Stephen Strange's morbid time loop
with Dormammu may have ended well for the

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.390
Master of the Mystic Arts.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:36.670
But Mordo's subsequent appearance as a villain-in-the-making
ensures that the good doctor's work in his

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.800
titular franchise is far from complete.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:45.790
Heck, even in a film like Infinity War, which
ends on a cliffhanger, the post-credit scene

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:50.870
with Nick Fury's pager still manages to throw
in one last dash of tension.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:55.920
And then you've got J. Jonah Jameson's Far
From Home appearance, which raises about a

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.700
million questions at once.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:04.050
All in all, one of the best ways that post-credit
scenes bring the heat is by regularly disrupting

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:09.720
the traditional concept of the "happy ending"
— and leaving audiences clamoring for more.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:16.150
Considering they exist in a detached form
at the tail-end of a movie, post-credit scenes

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:20.540
manage to get a lot of stuff done in a very
short amount of time.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:23.590
Take the new characters they introduce, for
example.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:28.300
When you look back over the years, there are
a ton of MCU favorites who interrupted a wall

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.280
of credits to make their first appearances.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.360
Nick Fury kicked the introductions off way
back at the MCU's genesis by popping up in

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.560
the final moments of Iron Man.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.090
Fans also met Wanda and Pietro Maximoff for
the first time at the end of Captain America:

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.970
The Winter Soldier, and Howard the Duck even
makes his first appearance in the Collector's

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:52.560
trashed museum at the end of Guardians of
the Galaxy.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.950
Sometimes the "introduction" comes with more
of a hint, though.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:01.170
Thor is preceded by Mjolnir at the end of
Iron Man 2, the long-awaited Adam Warlock

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:06.730
is strongly hinted at in a post-credit scene
for Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2, and of

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:12.200
course, Captain Marvel is headed off by her
famous symbol glowing on Nick Fury's pager

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.470
at the end of Infinity War.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:19.820
Whether it's the character themselves or a
blatant hint at their impending arrival, however,

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:25.390
Marvel uses their post-credit scenes to introduce
characters early and often throughout the

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:26.390
MCU.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:32.200
There's no doubt that each and every MCU film
has to juggle a lot of content.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:38.120
Multiple character arcs, storylines, and crossovers
must all be addressed in just a couple of

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.120
hours.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.560
This inevitably leaves countless ends loose
and questions unanswered as each story marches

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.380
towards its conclusion.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.590
With the larger interconnectedness of the
MCU to fall back on, these hints often aren't

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.640
addressed until later films.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:58.831
However, at times, the writers and directors
take advantage of the humble post-credit scene

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:04.350
to fill in some gaps, answer questions, and
occasionally even provide a big reveal or

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:07.390
two that never made it into the movie itself.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:11.500
Take, for instance, Erik Selvig's scene at
the end of Thor.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:17.200
With Odin back on the throne and peace apparently
restored to the Nine Realms, everything seems

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:23.210
hunky-dory in the son of Odin's world — except
for the little detail that Loki is using mind

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.580
control to manipulate Selvig.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:30.890
The big reveal sets up the first Avengers
film, of course, which follows two installments

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:32.150
later.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:37.040
Similar big reveals take place when Nick Fury
first brings up the Avengers Initiative in

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:43.500
Iron Man, Cap and Falcon detain Bucky at the
end of Ant-Man, and Wakanda finally gets some

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.030
real screen time in the final moments of Captain
America: Civil War.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:54.980
While many post-credit scenes are fairly forward-thinking,
occasionally, they serve as little more than

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:59.540
a dramatic flourish for the job-well-done-of-a-movie
that preceded them.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:05.680
The Guardians of the Galaxy franchise is particularly
good at pulling off these quirky embellishments.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.800
These movies consistently tapped into a variety
of entertaining credit scenes that feel like

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.140
nothing so much as a humorous bow at the end
of a successful third act.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:22.530
Baby Groot secretly dancing when Drax isn't
looking, Kraglin stabbing Drax with Yondu's

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:28.320
Arrow, and Peter Quill checking in on Adolescent
Groot to criticize the state of his bedroom

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.260
are all perfect codas to their respective
movies.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:36.320
They don't make grand gestures or reveal massive
new pieces of information.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.210
They're just plain fun.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.950
These kinds of scenes show up in other MCU
films, too.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:46.380
Captain America pops in for a quick public
service announcement at the end of Spider-Man:

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:47.710
Homecoming.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:51.840
Thor pays Jane Foster a visit at the end of
Thor: The Dark World.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.430
The team nabs a quick bite to eat at the end
of The Avengers.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:07:00.230
In all of these cases, the post-credit scenes
don't necessarily bring much in the way of

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:05.670
new info or future teases, but that doesn't
change the fact that they're pretty darn entertaining

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.050
all the same.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.530
One of the best ways that post-credit scenes
shine is simply by keeping the MCU freight

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.430
train rolling along.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:19.590
This is true whether a movie has an open-ended
resolution or wraps up in a neat and tidy

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:20.590
manner.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:25.180
In these cases, the post-credit scenes take
the audience by the shoulders, turn them in

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:31.440
a specific direction, and explicitly demonstrate
where the story is going next.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.820
Little hints of information, like Bucky checking
out his past at the end of The Winter Soldier

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:42.510
or Hope van Dyne being shown the Wasp suit
in Ant-Man's post-credit scene, are loaded

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:46.870
with possibility, but they don't actually
tell the viewer much.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:52.110
Meanwhile, post-credit scenes are often called
in to save the day by restoring a sense of

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:53.730
hope for the fans.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:59.140
Sometimes this is a direct counterstroke to
a devastating development, such as Nick Fury

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:05.800
paging Captain Marvel in the wake of Thanos'
snap, or Bucky being sent back into cryostasis

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.170
in Wakanda in order to finally find some rest
and healing.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:15.150
His future post-credit scene in Black Panther
also sowed the seeds of hope — that Bucky

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:19.780
would eventually find his place as a hero
in the Marvel universe.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:25.380
All this typically sets the internet forums
ablaze and gives podcasters and YouTubers

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:31.330
hours of juicy content, as they endlessly
rehash each scene and theorize about what

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:32.960
might happen next.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:38.289
Essentially, these scenes whet everyone's
appetites, prepare them for the future, and

00:08:38.289 --> 00:08:41.710
keep them coming back for more — time and
time again.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:47.540
The Mad Titan is a big deal in the world of
MCU post-credit scenes.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:52.110
After all, the villain practically lives on
the murky edges of the franchise for nearly

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:55.950
the entire first three quarters of the Infinity
Saga.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:09:01.730
Thanos' impending yet reticent off-screen
approach was dimly felt for years, as the

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:07.800
Infinity Stones were slowly revealed one by
one, and the storylines for Infinity War and

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.300
Endgame were gradually teed up.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:15.150
He plays a minor role in Guardians of the
Galaxy, sure, but apart from that, he only

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:19.360
appears twice before his full unveiling in
Infinity War.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.770
Where does he make these appearances, you
ask?

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:25.040
Why, in two post-credit sequences, of course.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:29.820
The Mad Titan first appeared at the end of
The Avengers, where he smiles at the thought

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:35.600
that challenging Earth's heroes would be courting
death — a shoutout to his morbidly romantic

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.089
comic book origins.

00:09:37.089 --> 00:09:42.550
He then appears once more in the middle of
the credits for Avengers: Age of Ultron, at

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:48.930
which point he dons the Infinity Gauntlet
and decides to take matters into his own hands.

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:54.480
While plenty of characters get their introductions
in post-credit sequences, Thanos alone can

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:00.360
claim that he was literally developed by them
for years — before he finally was unleashed

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:04.210
to wreak havoc on the main narrative itself.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.810
The Infinity Stones are a central part of
the MCU.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:12.750
This is especially true for the first three
phases that make up the Infinity Saga, which,

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:15.850
obviously, is literally named after them.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:20.830
The Tesseract shapes the stories of both Captain
Marvel and Captain America.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:25.060
The Power Stone is a central focal point for
the first Guardians film.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:30.320
The Mind Stone impacts the first two Avengers
movies and gives us Scarlet Witch, Quicksilver,

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:31.320
and Vision.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:38.130
The Aether also tampers with Thor's storyline,
and Doctor Strange would never have achieved

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.940
greatness without the help of the Eye of Agamotto.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:46.649
While most of the Infinity Stone storylines
take place in the main movies, though, the

00:10:46.649 --> 00:10:52.420
post-credit scenes do help fill in some pretty
important points in their collective story.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:57.130
The Tesseract's narrative thread goes right
through both Goose's upchucking credit scene

00:10:57.130 --> 00:11:01.810
in Captain Marvel to Erik Selvig's post-credit
scene in Thor.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:06.101
The Aether finds its way into the hands of
the Collector in his scene from Thor: The

00:11:06.101 --> 00:11:11.690
Dark World, and Wolfgang von Strucker's experiments
with the Mind Stone are first shown at the

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.370
end of The Winter Soldier.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:18.480
No matter which way you slice it, post-credit
scenes played a pivotal part in the Stones'

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.390
journeys throughout the Infinity Saga — and
it's fair to say they'll have a role to play

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:25.960
in MCU's next saga, too.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.570
Post-credit scenes are occasionally used to
provide a sense of closure that may have been

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.560
missing during the actual movie.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:38.800
For example, a good portion of Thor: Ragnarok
takes place on Sakaar, where Thor meets the

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:41.790
villainous Grandmaster and his cronies.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:47.270
Eventually, the newly-formed Revengers high-tail
it off the garbage planet, leaving a fledgling

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.050
rebellion in their wake.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:53.850
The movie itself doesn't show how that rebellion
plays out, though the audience is given a

00:11:53.850 --> 00:12:00.720
quick glimpse of Sakaar's fate when a post-credit
scene shows the Grandmaster cornered by rebels.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:06.899
Black Panther also ties up loose ends during
the credits, when a scene shows T'Challa informing

00:12:06.899 --> 00:12:12.339
the United Nations that Wakanda will be opening
up its borders — a theme that was brought

00:12:12.339 --> 00:12:14.920
up more than once throughout the film.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:18.020
Sometimes this closure is far more subtle,
though.

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:23.990
For instance, Avengers: Endgame famously lacked
a post-credit scene, opting instead to play

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:30.290
a few seconds of audio — that sounded an
awful lot like a final homage to Tony Stark,

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:33.270
hammering away on his Iron Man armor.

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:36.000
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:40.100
Plus, even more Looper videos about the MCU
are coming soon.

